---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Table from '../../../components/docs/Table.astro';
import { page, DOC_PAGES } from '../../../data/docs-nav';
import { DESTINATIONS, DELIVERY_FORMATS } from '../../../data/docs';

const entry = page('/docs/destinations/');

/** Pair each destination with its own page, so neither list can go stale. */
const cards = DESTINATIONS.map((destination) => ({
  ...destination,
  doc: DOC_PAGES.find((doc) => doc.href === `/docs/destinations/${destination.slug}/`)!,
}));

const formatName = (id: string) => DELIVERY_FORMATS.find((f) => f.id === id)?.name ?? id;
---

<DocsLayout page={entry} section="Destinations">
  <p>
    A destination is somewhere Hozz sends new Health records on its own. You can have several at
    once, each with its own set of Health types, its own format, its own schedule, and its own place
    in the queue — so a destination that is failing cannot hold up one that is working.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nothing is sent anywhere until you add one and confirm it. There is no default, because a
    default would be a place someone else chose.
  </p>

  <h2>The six</h2>

  <ul class="card-grid card-grid--2">
    {
      cards.map((destination) => (
        <li>
          <a class="doc-card" href={destination.doc.href}>
            <span class="doc-card-tag">
              {destination.recommended ? 'Recommended' : formatName(destination.defaultFormat)}
            </span>
            <h3>{destination.name}</h3>
            <p>{destination.summary}</p>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>Choosing between them</h2>

  <Table
    caption="Each destination, who it suits, and the format it starts with"
    columns={['Destination', 'Choose it when', 'Default format']}
  >
    {
      cards.map((destination) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            <a href={destination.doc.href}>{destination.name}</a>
          </th>
          <td>{destination.who}</td>
          <td>
            <code>{formatName(destination.defaultFormat)}</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <Callout kind="note" title="If you have no strong preference, pick a folder">
    <p>
      <a href="/docs/destinations/folder/">Folder</a> is the one Hozz recommends first, and it is the
      only one with no failure mode of its own. It needs no server, no open ports and no VPN, the sync
      client already on your computer does the networking, and it keeps working while that computer is
      switched off — which a push to a local receiver cannot.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Three of these are the same thing underneath</h2>

  <p>
    Home Assistant, InfluxDB and Web address are all HTTPS endpoints. They are listed separately on
    purpose: the most common question for a tool like this is "how do I connect it to Home
    Assistant", and answering "use the REST option, choose that format, and put this in that field"
    is the difference between a capability existing and a capability being found.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your Mac is an HTTPS endpoint too. It gets its own flow because Hozz discovers the computer over
    Bonjour and fills in the address and token for you, so you never have to know it was a web
    address.
  </p>

  <h2>What every destination has</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Health types.</strong> Limit a destination to the types you want it to receive, rather
      than everything.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A format.</strong> Five are available; which ones make sense depends on the
      destination. <a href="/docs/delivery-schema/">The delivery schema</a> documents all of them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A cadence.</strong> On arrival, hourly, daily, or manual only. These are requests —{' '}
      <a href="/docs/background-sync/">iOS decides when a background app runs</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Send a test.</strong> A small probe in the destination's own format, reporting exactly
      what came back. It contains no Health data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Its own cursor.</strong> A destination only advances past records it actually
      accepted.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Formats, briefly</h2>

  <Table
    caption="Delivery formats and what each keeps"
    columns={['Format', 'Media type', 'Keeps everything?']}
    mono
  >
    {
      DELIVERY_FORMATS.map((format) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{format.name}</th>
          <td>
            <code>{format.contentType}</code>
          </td>
          <td>{format.lossless ? 'Yes' : 'No — ' + format.note.split('. ').pop()}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <p>
    Neither SQLite nor Markdown is offered to a destination. A delivery is an append of new records,
    a database file is not appendable over HTTP, and a day's note rewritten from one batch would
    replace a whole day with a fraction of it. Both are available for{' '}
    <a href="/docs/formats/">manual export</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
